omap_uart: decode OMAP registers at the right offsets and widths

The OMAP-specific register block is mapped at base + 0x20, so the access
callbacks get offsets relative to that, but the switch uses offsets from
the UART base (MDR1 = 0x20, SCR = 0x40, SYSC = 0x54, SYSS = 0x58, ...).
MDR1 accesses landed in the default case ("Bad register 00000000"), SCR
writes went to MDR1, and SYSS/MVR read back 0.

32-bit accesses, which the OMAP2 L4 interconnect allows and Linux's hwmod
code uses for SYSC on every UART interrupt, went to omap_badwidth_*8(),
which forwards one byte to guest *physical* address = register offset:
address 0x54 is OneNAND (GPMC CS0), hence "onenand_write: unknown OneNAND
boot command". Accept 8/16/32-bit accesses; the registers are 8 bits wide.

QEMU 9.1.3: QEMU 9.1 still maps the region at base + 0x20 with cases at 0x20.

diff --git a/hw/char/omap_uart.c b/hw/char/omap_uart.c
index c2ef4c137..1f7040e7d 100644
--- a/hw/char/omap_uart.c
+++ b/hw/char/omap_uart.c
@@ -71,9 +71,7 @@ static uint64_t omap_uart_read(void *opaque, hwaddr addr, unsigned size)
 {
     struct omap_uart_s *s = opaque;
 
-    if (size == 4) {
-        return omap_badwidth_read8(opaque, addr);
-    }
+    addr += 0x20;	/* the region starts at MDR1 */
 
     switch (addr) {
     case 0x20:  /* MDR1 */
@@ -109,10 +107,8 @@ static void omap_uart_write(void *opaque, hwaddr addr,
 {
     struct omap_uart_s *s = opaque;
 
-    if (size == 4) {
-        omap_badwidth_write8(opaque, addr, value);
-        return;
-    }
+    addr += 0x20;	/* the region starts at MDR1 */
+    value &= 0xff;
 
     switch (addr) {
     case 0x20:  /* MDR1 */
